# FLIXO MVP Scope Contract

## 1. Dual-Workflow Execution Mandate

All end-user capabilities in FLIXO MUST support both workflows.

### AI Agent Guided Workflow
- Users can upload files and provide natural-language instructions.
- The AI Agent Router parses the request, resolves the required tool sequence, and triggers underlying client-side utility functions through tool chaining / function calling.
- If an instruction is ambiguous or execution fails, the Agent MUST gracefully fall back to suggesting the relevant manual tool.

### Manual Standalone Workflow
- Users retain full control to bypass the AI Agent.
- Every tool must be directly usable through a clean, intuitive UI.

## 2. Strict Client-Side Constraints

- 100% of file manipulation, compression, conversion, and text processing MUST execute locally in the user's browser.
- Permitted execution technologies include HTML5 Canvas, Web Workers, and WebAssembly (WASM).
- User files MUST NEVER leave the client browser or be transmitted to an external server.
- FLIXO execution MUST NOT depend on a backend.
- Hosting remains strictly static/CDN-based, such as Vercel or Cloudflare Pages.
- Once application assets/scripts are loaded, the execution path MUST work offline.

## 3. MVP Acceptance Criteria

A tool or feature may be certified for MVP only when all applicable criteria are satisfied:

- Agent Tool Registration: every standalone manual tool exposes a clean programmatic function interface reachable by the AI Agent Router.
- Deterministic Tool Chaining: the Agent Router achieves 100% measured execution accuracy for the defined standard-intent test suite when mapping intents to local browser function calls.
- Performance & Fallback: client-side processing does not freeze the main UI thread; Web Workers/WASM are used where applicable; fallback UI appears when AI routing fails.
- No Backend Dependency: the execution path works offline after application assets/scripts have been loaded.

## 4. Architectural Authority

The LLM is NOT the execution authority. It may interpret intent and propose a plan, but execution is performed by registered local capabilities through validated interfaces.

The canonical execution path is:

User Intent -> Agent Router -> Capability/Tool Registry -> Validation -> Local Client Executor -> Verification -> Result

Manual execution uses:

User -> Manual Tool UI -> Registered Local Executor -> Verification -> Result

## 5. Certification Rule

No feature is considered MVP-complete merely because source code exists. Certification requires executable evidence from the defined test suite, including deterministic routing, client-only execution, offline operation, UI responsiveness, and fallback behavior.

100% accuracy refers to the explicitly defined and versioned standard-intent test suite; it is a measurable acceptance criterion, not an unbounded claim about arbitrary natural-language input.

## Status

This file defines the mandatory FLIXO MVP scope contract and must be treated as an architectural constraint for future implementation and review.